\begin{enumerate}
    \item For continuous variables, probability density can take negative values.
    \item When rolling a die, the probability of getting 1 or 2 is 1/36.
    \item For discrete variables, probability can be expressed as the relative frequency taken over all possible events.
    \item The use of "probability" in expressions such as probability of precipitation includes subjective evaluation, and hence, is not a valid definition of probability.
    \item One way to express "unknown" using probability is to estimate the occurrence probability of all events as 0.
    \item The results of rolling a die or flipping a coin are mutually exclusive events.
    \item Probability mass can exceed 1.
    \item Probability density can exceed 1.
    \item The function that represents the probability distribution of a continuous variable is the probability density function.
    \item The function that represents the probability distribution of a discrete variable is the probability mass function.
\end{enumerate}
